WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.225 --> 00:00:04.230
- Hello Believe Nation,

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.253
I started the MentorMe series
with the goal to learn from

00:00:07.253 --> 00:00:09.014
people who've done a lot more than us,

00:00:09.014 --> 00:00:11.485
and by hanging around
them a little bit longer,

00:00:11.485 --> 00:00:13.234
some of their mindsets, their attitudes,

00:00:13.234 --> 00:00:16.010
their beliefs, the way
they think, seeps into us

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:19.596
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:19.596 --> 00:00:21.856
So today, we're going to
learn from Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:00:21.856 --> 00:00:26.252
pulling from his most
motivational clips from 2015.

00:00:26.252 --> 00:00:27.410
Mentor me, Gary.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.326
And as usual, guys, as Gary is talking,

00:00:29.326 --> 00:00:31.581
if he says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:31.581 --> 00:00:33.296
please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:00:33.296 --> 00:00:34.273
Put quotes around it

00:00:34.273 --> 00:00:36.787
so that other people
can be inspired as well.

00:00:36.787 --> 00:00:37.784
Enjoy!

00:00:37.784 --> 00:00:41.701
(uplifting instrumental music)

00:00:50.348 --> 00:00:51.743
- I hear people say, like, well,

00:00:51.743 --> 00:00:53.015
how do I get in New York Times,

00:00:53.015 --> 00:00:55.926
or how do I get that
meeting with that CEO,

00:00:55.926 --> 00:01:00.093
and oftentimes, I just say
one is better than zero.

00:01:04.514 --> 00:01:07.744
The steps it takes to actually get to

00:01:07.744 --> 00:01:09.339
the biggest places in the world,

00:01:09.339 --> 00:01:11.497
before you get a meeting with, you know,

00:01:11.497 --> 00:01:15.332
Zucks, or Cuban, or Barry
Diller, or whoever you're

00:01:15.332 --> 00:01:17.176
trying to have a meeting
with in the business world,

00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:18.733
well, you've got to have
a lot of little meetings

00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:20.109
to build up your cadence.

00:01:20.109 --> 00:01:21.000
People are always like,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.005
man, you've been on Conan, and Ellen,

00:01:24.005 --> 00:01:26.971
and the Today Show, and CNN,

00:01:26.971 --> 00:01:29.744
and Fox, like, how do I get that?

00:01:29.744 --> 00:01:33.194
And I'm like, well, I also
did a thousand interviews,

00:01:33.194 --> 00:01:38.183
it feels like, on videos that
got one or 19 or 137 views.

00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:41.373
Like, my PR people and all
my managers and handlers

00:01:41.373 --> 00:01:43.396
are really pissed at me,
because they're like,

00:01:43.396 --> 00:01:45.272
why are you spending 15 minutes

00:01:45.272 --> 00:01:48.202
doing these blogs that have six readers,

00:01:48.202 --> 00:01:49.738
you know, every single day,

00:01:49.738 --> 00:01:52.000
and you're passing on the
stuff that we're giving you

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.603
to be on CNN or New York Times?

00:01:53.603 --> 00:01:56.798
I still, even today, am as happy,

00:01:56.798 --> 00:01:58.201
and sometimes even more excited,

00:01:58.201 --> 00:02:00.913
to be on a podcast or a video
blog that's only going to reach

00:02:00.913 --> 00:02:03.905
100 people, because I'm always
about depth versus width.

00:02:03.905 --> 00:02:05.887
Do want to go wide, or
do you want to go deep.

00:02:05.887 --> 00:02:07.450
I want to go deeper with my community.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.007
I wanted to give back to
people that were fans of me.

00:02:10.007 --> 00:02:12.732
And so even today, where I
have more leverage than I did

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:14.313
three or four or five, six years ago,

00:02:14.313 --> 00:02:17.752
to get bigger platforms, I
still work under the model of

00:02:17.752 --> 00:02:19.394
one is better than zero.

00:02:19.394 --> 00:02:21.937
To me, doing these interviews
is the process of the work

00:02:21.937 --> 00:02:25.215
that so many people are
impatient and not willing to do.

00:02:25.215 --> 00:02:28.319
Are you willing to take ten
minutes to get 195 views?

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:29.152
I am.

00:02:29.152 --> 00:02:30.389
I don't know who you think you are.

00:02:30.389 --> 00:02:32.965
When you've not gotten the exposure yet,

00:02:32.965 --> 00:02:35.934
when you have not made your name just yet,

00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:37.537
when you're still making the climb,

00:02:37.537 --> 00:02:40.055
when you're not paying
attention 'cause nobody else is

00:02:40.055 --> 00:02:41.929
'cause 217 people are watching the video,

00:02:41.929 --> 00:02:43.747
I'm grinding when you're sleeping.

00:02:43.747 --> 00:02:47.913
I did those 87s and 59s
and 137s and 813 views

00:02:47.913 --> 00:02:49.445
day in and day out, day in and day out,

00:02:49.445 --> 00:02:51.522
day in and day out, and continued to.

00:02:51.522 --> 00:02:53.588
You may make a video on
somebody's video show

00:02:53.588 --> 00:02:57.289
that has 89 viewers, but
one of those 89 viewers

00:02:57.289 --> 00:03:01.374
may be a producer at CNN,
or, or, might be the CFO

00:03:01.374 --> 00:03:03.461
of a big company that
you're trying to reach,

00:03:03.461 --> 00:03:05.713
and so undervaluing just that one view,

00:03:05.713 --> 00:03:08.237
it needs to be the right
view, but it's one view,

00:03:08.237 --> 00:03:09.566
is a humongous mistake.

00:03:09.566 --> 00:03:11.922
It's about having the humility.

00:03:11.922 --> 00:03:15.040
It's about not saying no,
even when you've made it.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.403
And the one is greater than zero concept

00:03:18.403 --> 00:03:21.059
is something I'm proud that
I continue to execute on.

00:03:21.059 --> 00:03:23.494
If you actually love what you're doing,

00:03:23.494 --> 00:03:25.161
if you actually love it,

00:03:25.161 --> 00:03:27.367
there's no friction to turn on your brain.

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:29.536
The only time I feel like
I have to turn on my brain

00:03:29.536 --> 00:03:32.369
was when I was six to 22 years old

00:03:33.451 --> 00:03:34.927
when I was going through the bullsh--

00:03:34.927 --> 00:03:36.777
education system of America.

00:03:36.777 --> 00:03:37.610
Right?

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.297
That's when I felt like I
had to turn it on, you know,

00:03:39.297 --> 00:03:43.100
to appease horse crap that
didn't match my reality,

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.708
but ever since that day, when like, yay!

00:03:45.708 --> 00:03:48.255
And I went into like, you
need to buy this pinot grigio.

00:03:48.255 --> 00:03:50.578
The second that started in May of 1998,

00:03:50.578 --> 00:03:53.005
there's never been a day
that I've had to activate.

00:03:53.005 --> 00:03:54.511
It's always on.

00:03:54.511 --> 00:03:57.635
And I don't mean always-on
buzzword marketing, it's that

00:03:57.635 --> 00:04:00.820
I love what I do so much
that there is no friction

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.506
to turn it on, even when I
am landing at two o'clock

00:04:03.506 --> 00:04:05.214
in the morning from a
flight that's delayed

00:04:05.214 --> 00:04:06.342
like the other night,

00:04:06.342 --> 00:04:08.971
and then going directly into
it at 6:30 in the morning,

00:04:08.971 --> 00:04:12.690
because the fire is so deep
inside, you love it so much.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:14.503
You know, you don't need that jumpstart.

00:04:14.503 --> 00:04:17.536
- [COLSAUCE] Aimee asks, a
professor once said to me,

00:04:17.536 --> 00:04:19.912
"It's better to be a big
fish in a small pond."

00:04:19.912 --> 00:04:20.995
Do you agree?

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:26.037
- Aimee, this, you know,
I'm going to be very upfront

00:04:26.037 --> 00:04:27.163
on this question.

00:04:27.163 --> 00:04:29.080
This is a good way to bang out the show.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:31.984
That sounds like a loser professor to me.

00:04:31.984 --> 00:04:35.170
It's small-time thinking,
I don't subscribe to it,

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.293
there's a lot of practicality in it,

00:04:37.293 --> 00:04:38.405
right, there's...

00:04:38.405 --> 00:04:40.060
It's a lot easier to be the
most successful businessman

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:43.371
in St. Louis, Missouri, than
it is to be in the world,

00:04:43.371 --> 00:04:45.537
right, so I understand the thesis,

00:04:45.537 --> 00:04:48.972
but honestly, that, to
me, is very conservative,

00:04:48.972 --> 00:04:50.628
not in my DNA thinking.

00:04:50.628 --> 00:04:52.806
That could be great
advice for a lot of people

00:04:52.806 --> 00:04:55.784
who are delirious and think
they're better than they are,

00:04:55.784 --> 00:04:58.735
though I love to drive through
self-esteem and optimism,

00:04:58.735 --> 00:05:01.042
and I think early in your life,

00:05:01.042 --> 00:05:02.191
you should absolutely shoot for

00:05:02.191 --> 00:05:03.755
the highest stars that you can.

00:05:03.755 --> 00:05:06.753
I think as life moves
on, and time moves on,

00:05:06.753 --> 00:05:09.248
you need to become more practical,

00:05:09.248 --> 00:05:11.112
you've got bills and kids,

00:05:11.112 --> 00:05:14.303
and all those kind of things,
but to start off one's career

00:05:14.303 --> 00:05:16.978
in the first 20 years,
call it 'til you're 40,

00:05:16.978 --> 00:05:19.182
to be in that thinking,
I think that limits.

00:05:19.182 --> 00:05:21.732
There have been so many
people who've hedged

00:05:21.732 --> 00:05:24.928
and settled in their lives, and honestly,

00:05:24.928 --> 00:05:26.987
I'm not the kind of
character that's going to

00:05:26.987 --> 00:05:28.609
play in that kind of sphere.

00:05:28.609 --> 00:05:29.769
I'm going in a different direction,

00:05:29.769 --> 00:05:30.925
it's what comes natural to me,

00:05:30.925 --> 00:05:32.534
but it's also because I think I can be

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:35.091
the biggest and the
baddest in any game I play.

00:05:35.091 --> 00:05:36.438
It's just how I'm wired.

00:05:36.438 --> 00:05:37.988
I think this comes down to...

00:05:37.988 --> 00:05:40.190
It's probably very good advice to some,

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:42.187
and it's probably
atrocious advice to others,

00:05:42.187 --> 00:05:44.644
and this is why I continue
to say, if I could inject

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:47.762
anything into anybody, it
would be self-awareness,

00:05:47.762 --> 00:05:50.799
because it would help you
answer this question, because

00:05:50.799 --> 00:05:53.329
for a lot of people, it's
probably really solid advice.

00:05:53.329 --> 00:05:55.858
For me, and for many of you watching this,

00:05:55.858 --> 00:05:57.368
it's crap advice.

00:05:57.368 --> 00:05:59.977
Being a dick about it. I
know I'm sitting in a room

00:05:59.977 --> 00:06:02.108
full of number two's, three's, and four's.

00:06:02.108 --> 00:06:04.602
There's so many people in
this room that are like,

00:06:04.602 --> 00:06:07.059
going to be dominant,
like, could be dominant,

00:06:07.059 --> 00:06:10.025
number two, three's, and
four's, and I'm pissed

00:06:10.025 --> 00:06:12.141
that the narrative now is that
you need to be a number one

00:06:12.141 --> 00:06:14.131
and have your company, because
a lot of people are going to

00:06:14.131 --> 00:06:16.339
lose or leave money on the table...

00:06:16.339 --> 00:06:18.597
Because that's just the narrative.

00:06:18.597 --> 00:06:22.068
I really do think you have to
audit who you actually are.

00:06:22.068 --> 00:06:24.498
You can look at like, how people roll,

00:06:24.498 --> 00:06:27.055
and like, it's great to admire
and things of that nature,

00:06:27.055 --> 00:06:29.104
but it's so damn important.

00:06:29.104 --> 00:06:31.234
You need to bet on your strengths,

00:06:31.234 --> 00:06:33.328
and don't give a f---
about what you suck at.

00:06:33.328 --> 00:06:34.592
Way too many people in this
room are going to spend

00:06:34.592 --> 00:06:36.007
the next 30, 40 years of their lives

00:06:36.007 --> 00:06:38.030
trying to check the
boxes of the things that

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.131
they're not as good at,

00:06:39.131 --> 00:06:42.053
and that, you're going to waste
a f---load of time and lose.

00:06:42.053 --> 00:06:45.184
To stick to like, your
DNA, because the truth is,

00:06:45.184 --> 00:06:48.936
if you want to be an anomaly,
you've got to act like one.

00:06:48.936 --> 00:06:50.323
You know, like...

00:06:50.323 --> 00:06:52.038
And so, that's it, that's what I got.

00:06:52.038 --> 00:06:53.367
So thanks for having me.

00:06:53.367 --> 00:06:55.189
Action or enthusiasm.

00:06:55.189 --> 00:06:58.126
The truth is, my friend, m squared,

00:06:58.126 --> 00:06:59.994
can I call you that, Mister Money,

00:06:59.994 --> 00:07:03.872
the real answer to that
question really is, both.

00:07:03.872 --> 00:07:06.276
The funny thing is, I know tons of people

00:07:06.276 --> 00:07:10.595
with massive enthusiasm,
and they can't execute sh--.

00:07:10.595 --> 00:07:13.834
On the flip side, I know
people that can execute A's on

00:07:13.834 --> 00:07:17.552
all their papers, but without
that passion or bravado,

00:07:17.552 --> 00:07:20.990
or fire, they're only
able to get limited return

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:24.059
on that investment of execution, so to me,

00:07:24.059 --> 00:07:26.138
I'm probably a 60/40,

00:07:26.138 --> 00:07:28.378
action/execution 60,

00:07:28.378 --> 00:07:29.516
enthusiasm 40.

00:07:29.516 --> 00:07:31.271
I know a lot of people might not get that,

00:07:31.271 --> 00:07:33.494
but the truth is, I'm way more proud

00:07:33.494 --> 00:07:35.564
of my execution skills.

00:07:35.564 --> 00:07:38.064
All this, all this charisma...

00:07:38.942 --> 00:07:43.552
Hides what I really am,
which a hardcore executer.

00:07:43.552 --> 00:07:46.142
Don't forget, I had
executed a large business

00:07:46.142 --> 00:07:48.087
before I ever hit the scene.

00:07:48.087 --> 00:07:50.624
I was already 30 years old when I started

00:07:50.624 --> 00:07:53.782
Wine Library TV, and
started that whole process,

00:07:53.782 --> 00:07:58.347
and so, I'm a 60/40
execution, enthusiasm, action.

00:07:58.347 --> 00:08:00.778
I might even be a 65/35.

00:08:00.778 --> 00:08:04.379
Heck, as I sit here, maybe I'm a 70/30.

00:08:04.379 --> 00:08:06.607
What I would say the hustle is to me

00:08:06.607 --> 00:08:09.056
is that when you have
passion around something,

00:08:09.056 --> 00:08:10.507
that you are squeezing

00:08:10.507 --> 00:08:14.385
every last bit of the
juice out of the orange.

00:08:14.385 --> 00:08:15.218
Right?

00:08:15.218 --> 00:08:18.325
To me, hustle is maximizing the energy

00:08:18.325 --> 00:08:19.393
you're putting into somebody.

00:08:19.393 --> 00:08:22.716
I'm blown away by people
saying that they're hustling,

00:08:22.716 --> 00:08:24.887
and they want to achieve
these great things,

00:08:24.887 --> 00:08:26.949
and then their actions don't match.

00:08:26.949 --> 00:08:28.883
It's like saying you
really want to lose weight

00:08:28.883 --> 00:08:31.262
while eating a Big Mac, right?

00:08:31.262 --> 00:08:34.759
So to me, hustle would be
putting all your effort

00:08:34.759 --> 00:08:37.724
into achieving the goal
at hand, and for me,

00:08:37.724 --> 00:08:40.622
that means making every minute count.

00:08:40.622 --> 00:08:44.026
Hiring a full-time
assistant who is, I mean,

00:08:44.026 --> 00:08:46.769
Friday night didn't happen
because I was hustling.

00:08:46.769 --> 00:08:49.014
Like, every minute, like,
if I miss my 15 minutes

00:08:49.014 --> 00:08:51.336
to tape the show, I've
missed my 15 minutes,

00:08:51.336 --> 00:08:53.004
because there's so much hustle in my day.

00:08:53.004 --> 00:08:55.976
There's no, you know, there's no like...

00:08:55.976 --> 00:08:58.106
There's no hanging out.

00:08:58.106 --> 00:08:59.702
There's no like time where I like...

00:08:59.702 --> 00:09:01.647
If I get to do like one joke with you guys

00:09:01.647 --> 00:09:02.938
as I'm walking in between meetings,

00:09:02.938 --> 00:09:05.536
we like find that to be a
rare acquaintance situation,

00:09:05.536 --> 00:09:08.516
like, like, occurrence, not acquaintance.

00:09:08.516 --> 00:09:10.398
Hustle is putting it all on the line.

00:09:10.398 --> 00:09:12.582
Hustle is waking up one day,

00:09:12.582 --> 00:09:15.134
the day before you die, and
realizing you gave your all,

00:09:15.134 --> 00:09:16.751
into the parenting of your children,

00:09:16.751 --> 00:09:18.155
the building of your businesses,

00:09:18.155 --> 00:09:20.577
the philanthropy that you wanted to do,

00:09:20.577 --> 00:09:24.889
whatever you define, it's
just, you know, all in.

00:09:24.889 --> 00:09:26.862
Emotionally and executionally,

00:09:26.862 --> 00:09:29.718
in theory and in strategy,
and in execution.

00:09:29.718 --> 00:09:31.628
My dad taught me word is bond.

00:09:31.628 --> 00:09:32.977
I've said it a couple times for the people

00:09:32.977 --> 00:09:34.667
that watch me a lot, they know this,

00:09:34.667 --> 00:09:37.318
that when I walked into
my dad's business at 14,

00:09:37.318 --> 00:09:39.800
let's just say I was a little
bit of a bullsh-- artist.

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:41.440
And, you know, a lot of the charisma

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.123
and the showmanship and the salesmanship

00:09:43.123 --> 00:09:45.141
that a lot of you enjoy,
and I appreciate that,

00:09:45.141 --> 00:09:47.409
also comes with being full of crap.

00:09:47.409 --> 00:09:49.538
It's got a very similar cousin DNA,

00:09:49.538 --> 00:09:51.192
and I'm glad, I got very lucky,

00:09:51.192 --> 00:09:52.150
let's call it what it is,

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:55.291
I do not think I'd be as
honorable or as good of a dude

00:09:55.291 --> 00:09:58.278
without my dad's correction
of, you make a commitment...

00:09:58.278 --> 00:09:59.468
I'll never forget it.

00:09:59.468 --> 00:10:00.815
You make a commitment, you buy 50...

00:10:00.815 --> 00:10:01.747
When I started buying wine.

00:10:01.747 --> 00:10:05.531
You buy 50 cases, no matter
what, you take those 50 cases.

00:10:05.531 --> 00:10:06.902
If you say I'll take those 50 cases.

00:10:06.902 --> 00:10:08.165
If the market changes,

00:10:08.165 --> 00:10:10.267
if it gets an 84 in the Wine Spectator,

00:10:10.267 --> 00:10:12.693
you take it, you eat it, you drink it,

00:10:12.693 --> 00:10:14.854
your word is bond, and
because I play the marathon,

00:10:14.854 --> 00:10:17.184
not the sprint, long-term businessman,

00:10:17.184 --> 00:10:19.686
that's helped me a lot,
because I've kept relationships

00:10:19.686 --> 00:10:24.259
forever because sticking to
it has mattered quite a bit.

00:10:24.259 --> 00:10:26.770
I think that I paint a
narrative of me wanting

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:29.694
to buy the Jets for 30 years,
that when I buy the Jets,

00:10:29.694 --> 00:10:31.683
everybody's going to freak the f--- out.

00:10:31.683 --> 00:10:32.516
(audience laughs)

00:10:32.516 --> 00:10:33.524
Yeah, right?

00:10:33.524 --> 00:10:34.460
And so like, and that, I'm sure,

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:36.274
on some level keeps me
motivated, you know,

00:10:36.274 --> 00:10:37.457
there's a real dirty secret.

00:10:37.457 --> 00:10:39.245
I'll go deep on you guys here, like,

00:10:39.245 --> 00:10:41.237
I don't give a f--- if
I buy the Jets, right?

00:10:41.237 --> 00:10:43.029
Like, I love the process.

00:10:43.029 --> 00:10:46.780
I made a video four years
ago that sits in the cloud,

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:48.624
hopefully it doesn't disappear,

00:10:48.624 --> 00:10:50.926
where I literally talk about, I actually,

00:10:50.926 --> 00:10:52.847
this is the worst day of my life,

00:10:52.847 --> 00:10:55.207
and it's the whole narrative
of the day I buy the Jets,

00:10:55.207 --> 00:10:59.246
how upset I am, because
I love the climb, right?

00:10:59.246 --> 00:11:01.643
And that's what I'm here for.

00:11:01.643 --> 00:11:03.012
Like, that's why I'm even here.

00:11:03.012 --> 00:11:03.845
I love the climb.

00:11:03.845 --> 00:11:06.082
I love, like, talking
out stuff with peeps,

00:11:06.082 --> 00:11:08.079
like I love the climb, I love the process,

00:11:08.079 --> 00:11:10.256
and by creating such a heavy North Star,

00:11:10.256 --> 00:11:13.819
you know, it's creating a
game where I can, you know,

00:11:13.819 --> 00:11:16.961
keep climbing without ever
really having to change it.

00:11:16.961 --> 00:11:19.421
I feel like resetting your North Star,

00:11:19.421 --> 00:11:21.974
I'm a very big, shoot for
the moon, land in the clouds,

00:11:21.974 --> 00:11:24.121
I feel like always having that North Star,

00:11:24.121 --> 00:11:27.175
it has made me a much more
honorable, legacy-driven

00:11:27.175 --> 00:11:30.280
entrepreneur, because I'm
trying to go for this big thing,

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:32.634
and a lot of things are going
to happen in the next 40 years,

00:11:32.634 --> 00:11:36.618
so skimming an extra 50k as a
salesman isn't going to help me,

00:11:36.618 --> 00:11:38.722
you know, because that person
might help me in 13 years

00:11:38.722 --> 00:11:42.480
when they're xyz, and so,
I think it is a mechanism

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:44.230
that I created for a
couple different reasons:

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:47.726
one, the Jets represented,
to me, becoming American.

00:11:47.726 --> 00:11:48.559
And, in a weird way,

00:11:48.559 --> 00:11:50.586
it's like the ultimate Kickstarter, right?

00:11:50.586 --> 00:11:52.916
Like my real plan is, if I'm like 75,

00:11:52.916 --> 00:11:54.200
and I feel like I can't get there,

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:56.099
I'm just going to ask
everybody to help me.

00:11:56.099 --> 00:11:56.932
(audience laughs)

00:11:56.932 --> 00:11:58.219
I feel like if I can infect enough people

00:11:58.219 --> 00:12:00.121
with the narrative, everybody's
going to want to kick in

00:12:00.121 --> 00:12:01.780
their five, three, eight cents.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:04.282
I'm a big fan of going
all in on your strengths

00:12:04.282 --> 00:12:07.040
and outsourcing or not
focusing on your weaknesses.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:09.132
I, I think you've got it pegged right.

00:12:09.132 --> 00:12:10.635
I think the one thing is...

00:12:10.635 --> 00:12:12.642
Sometimes it's obvious, right?

00:12:12.642 --> 00:12:14.285
Don't get caught in the obvious,

00:12:14.285 --> 00:12:17.024
meaning, it's obvious to you
that you suck at accounting.

00:12:17.024 --> 00:12:18.485
It's kind of easy, right, it's math...

00:12:18.485 --> 00:12:20.633
You probably also don't
want to or like doing it.

00:12:20.633 --> 00:12:21.719
That's easy.

00:12:21.719 --> 00:12:25.154
It's the thing that's in the
shadows, that's not as obvious.

00:12:25.154 --> 00:12:26.395
There's something you're doing right now

00:12:26.395 --> 00:12:28.318
that a lot of people
could do better than you.

00:12:28.318 --> 00:12:30.123
It's easy when you're an F at it,

00:12:30.123 --> 00:12:31.468
and you know you need an A.

00:12:31.468 --> 00:12:33.282
But what about the fact that
you might be doing something

00:12:33.282 --> 00:12:34.762
right now at Tiger or somewhere else

00:12:34.762 --> 00:12:36.576
where you're a B at it,

00:12:36.576 --> 00:12:39.493
but you could afford to
have somebody be an A at it.

00:12:39.493 --> 00:12:41.259
That's where people miss the mistakes.

00:12:41.259 --> 00:12:42.910
So, it takes deep self-awareness.

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:45.890
You know, I'm not a big
believer in like, try, try,

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:48.222
try harder, be better
at this thing, I think

00:12:48.222 --> 00:12:51.044
it's a waste of resources,
energy, and happiness often.

00:12:51.044 --> 00:12:53.250
Now, if you get off on that kind
of thing, like, persevering,

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:54.083
knock yourself out.

00:12:54.083 --> 00:12:54.916
Roll how you want to roll.

00:12:54.916 --> 00:12:57.333
But for me, big believer in
betting on all your strengths

00:12:57.333 --> 00:13:00.898
and trying to find complements
to your weaknesses.

00:13:00.898 --> 00:13:03.074
- [WHoltzman] Young Entrepreneur asks,

00:13:03.074 --> 00:13:05.351
would you ever automate your position,

00:13:05.351 --> 00:13:07.659
delegate to as many people as it takes,

00:13:07.659 --> 00:13:11.826
and spend 95% of your time fully
engaged in family and life?

00:13:13.024 --> 00:13:14.249
- Absolutely not.

00:13:14.249 --> 00:13:15.877
Is this Young Entrepreneur?

00:13:15.877 --> 00:13:17.582
Young Entrepreneur, let
me tell you something

00:13:17.582 --> 00:13:18.932
from Old Entrepreneur,

00:13:18.932 --> 00:13:21.565
which is I love the process of the work.

00:13:21.565 --> 00:13:22.544
I love the grind.

00:13:22.544 --> 00:13:23.772
I love the climb.

00:13:23.772 --> 00:13:26.986
You know, I love the idea of
getting to buying the Jets

00:13:26.986 --> 00:13:28.185
moreso than buying the Jets.

00:13:28.185 --> 00:13:29.437
It's the process.

00:13:29.437 --> 00:13:32.517
To delegate out what I love
would defeat the purpose.

00:13:32.517 --> 00:13:34.636
Of course I want to spend
as much time with my family

00:13:34.636 --> 00:13:37.537
as possible, but that
would be lying to my soul,

00:13:37.537 --> 00:13:39.984
if I didn't do what I want to do,

00:13:39.984 --> 00:13:43.007
and what I want to do for a
living is build organizations,

00:13:43.007 --> 00:13:46.115
build companies, create
commerce, be a salesman,

00:13:46.115 --> 00:13:49.301
and be a guidance counselor, HR character.

00:13:49.301 --> 00:13:51.737
Social commentate and put out content.

00:13:51.737 --> 00:13:54.845
I would suffocate if I couldn't put out

00:13:54.845 --> 00:13:58.835
the work that's needed to
accomplish the things that I want.

00:13:58.835 --> 00:13:59.668
Psst!

00:13:59.668 --> 00:14:00.824
The problem, Young Entrepreneur,

00:14:00.824 --> 00:14:02.036
especially when you're
a young entrepreneur

00:14:02.036 --> 00:14:03.629
is people want stuff, right?

00:14:03.629 --> 00:14:05.791
They want the bling bling here, right,

00:14:05.791 --> 00:14:08.374
they want the cool-ass kicks,

00:14:08.374 --> 00:14:10.925
they want the car, they want the jet,

00:14:10.925 --> 00:14:11.759
not the Jets.

00:14:11.759 --> 00:14:12.797
They want stuff.

00:14:12.797 --> 00:14:13.636
I don't want stuff.

00:14:13.636 --> 00:14:16.795
I want the sweat and the
pain and the gratitude

00:14:16.795 --> 00:14:21.359
and the, you know, happiness
that comes along with the work.

00:14:21.359 --> 00:14:22.192
I want the work.

00:14:22.192 --> 00:14:24.109
I don't want the stuff.

00:14:25.518 --> 00:14:27.172
- Thank you guys so much for watching!

00:14:27.172 --> 00:14:28.260
I hope you enjoyed.

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:29.254
I'd love to know

00:14:29.254 --> 00:14:32.741
what got you fired up
most about this video.

00:14:32.741 --> 00:14:35.226
It's hard not to be motivated
after watching this one,

00:14:35.226 --> 00:14:36.663
but what was your favorite clip,

00:14:36.663 --> 00:14:38.190
what did you take from
this that you're then

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:41.053
immediately going to pour into
your business and your life?

00:14:41.053 --> 00:14:42.895
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:14:42.895 --> 00:14:44.275
Super curious to find out.

00:14:44.275 --> 00:14:46.648
I also want to give a quick
shout-out to César Bonilla,

00:14:46.648 --> 00:14:48.861
thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:14:48.861 --> 00:14:51.037
Your One Word, and
tweeting that picture out

00:14:51.037 --> 00:14:51.995
about it as well.

00:14:51.995 --> 00:14:53.904
I really, really, really,
really appreciate it.

00:14:53.904 --> 00:14:55.352
So thank you guys again for watching!

00:14:55.352 --> 00:14:58.780
I believe in you, I hope you
continue to believe in yourself

00:14:58.780 --> 00:15:00.906
and whatever your one word is.

00:15:00.906 --> 00:15:03.619
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:15:03.619 --> 00:15:06.952
(air rushes forcefully)

00:15:09.599 --> 00:15:10.650
- [Steve_Campbell] Roberto asks,

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:13.111
what do you feel is a
bigger obstacle to success,

00:15:13.111 --> 00:15:16.616
a lack of time, or a lack of capital?

00:15:16.616 --> 00:15:18.517
- Roberto, this is a tremendous question.

00:15:18.517 --> 00:15:21.859
I think the biggest obstacle to success

00:15:21.859 --> 00:15:23.692
is a lack of optimism.

00:15:25.018 --> 00:15:28.110
That question in itself
is the problem, my friend.

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:28.943
Right?

00:15:28.943 --> 00:15:31.056
You're looking at two things
that are both negatives,

00:15:31.056 --> 00:15:33.266
and guess what, both
of them are obstacles.

00:15:33.266 --> 00:15:36.309
When I started winelibrary.com
transformation for my

00:15:36.309 --> 00:15:39.038
business, I had time I worked
my face off every minute,

00:15:39.038 --> 00:15:40.278
but we didn't have a whole lot of money

00:15:40.278 --> 00:15:42.489
in our profit center, so
it took more time, right?

00:15:42.489 --> 00:15:43.537
It's just the way it is.

00:15:43.537 --> 00:15:46.323
Today, I have more money,
but boy, don't I have time,

00:15:46.323 --> 00:15:50.771
but neither, ever, ever
will be an excuse for me,

00:15:50.771 --> 00:15:54.116
and so, just to drill
this through the throat

00:15:54.116 --> 00:15:55.871
of the Vayner nation, that's right,

00:15:55.871 --> 00:15:58.564
I went that graphic, don't smile, D-Roc.

00:15:58.564 --> 00:15:59.798
Here's the bottom line.

00:15:59.798 --> 00:16:03.614
I refuse to allow you to get
an answer to the question

00:16:03.614 --> 00:16:06.677
because both of them are firmly square

00:16:06.677 --> 00:16:09.953
in the excuse column, and I
have no patience for that.

00:16:09.953 --> 00:16:11.262
There will always be problems.

00:16:11.262 --> 00:16:13.519
Let's talk about a million other things

00:16:13.519 --> 00:16:16.436
that are a way to stop success.

00:16:16.436 --> 00:16:18.765
The health and wellbeing
of your family members,

00:16:18.765 --> 00:16:21.574
so it takes your mind away from execution.

00:16:21.574 --> 00:16:23.676
The country you live in's government

00:16:23.676 --> 00:16:27.560
and political, you know,
concepts at these moments,

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:30.107
a la startups in China
that I've invested in

00:16:30.107 --> 00:16:32.084
that got traction, but
then people that were

00:16:32.084 --> 00:16:35.048
wired into the government
decided to not allow it

00:16:35.048 --> 00:16:36.977
to happen, and then the
startup disappeared.

00:16:36.977 --> 00:16:38.437
Not as easy to be an entrepreneur there.

00:16:38.437 --> 00:16:39.899
It's still a communist country.

00:16:39.899 --> 00:16:41.428
Sorry, it just is.

00:16:41.428 --> 00:16:43.911
And so all these things
can be problems, right?

00:16:43.911 --> 00:16:46.560
There's a competitor
with a billion dollars

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:49.476
who's also skilled and
punches you in the mouth

00:16:49.476 --> 00:16:51.335
and knocks you out in the first round.

00:16:51.335 --> 00:16:52.168
Right?

00:16:52.168 --> 00:16:53.145
The world changing.

00:16:53.145 --> 00:16:55.902
I mean, there's just a
million obstacles, right?

00:16:55.902 --> 00:16:56.773
The media.

00:16:56.773 --> 00:16:58.360
One bad coverage of you.

00:16:58.360 --> 00:16:59.777
A moment in time.

00:17:00.713 --> 00:17:02.381
You know when I think about it a lot?

00:17:02.381 --> 00:17:03.347
You know what I think about a lot?

00:17:03.347 --> 00:17:04.298
Let's get really real.

00:17:04.298 --> 00:17:05.597
This is why we did this show.

00:17:05.597 --> 00:17:09.834
I always, I'm a human being,
and I always think about

00:17:09.834 --> 00:17:10.893
a moment in time.

00:17:10.893 --> 00:17:14.151
What if I just say the wrong
thing at the wrong time.

00:17:14.151 --> 00:17:14.984
Right?

00:17:14.984 --> 00:17:17.005
What if I call out China for
being a communist country

00:17:17.005 --> 00:17:19.799
in an episode while I'm
on a rant, and somebody

00:17:19.799 --> 00:17:21.481
who's watching doesn't
like the way that tastes,

00:17:21.481 --> 00:17:23.839
and it takes away a business
opportunity for me in China

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:26.286
in seven years, even though
I'm not trying to zing,

00:17:26.286 --> 00:17:27.234
it's just things that I saw.

00:17:27.234 --> 00:17:29.805
What if, what if, you know, what if...

00:17:29.805 --> 00:17:32.824
What if I look down on my
phone while I'm driving,

00:17:32.824 --> 00:17:35.327
even though I've really not
done that, and I hit somebody,

00:17:35.327 --> 00:17:37.758
and I kill them, and
that becomes the story.

00:17:37.758 --> 00:17:39.281
And like, forget about the story,

00:17:39.281 --> 00:17:40.240
about what you think of me.

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:43.192
I will never recover from
that, because I killed somebody

00:17:43.192 --> 00:17:44.649
because I needed to check a tweet.

00:17:44.649 --> 00:17:46.226
These are moments in time.

00:17:46.226 --> 00:17:48.255
So there are so many things

00:17:48.255 --> 00:17:50.764
that can keep you from
being successful, right?

00:17:50.764 --> 00:17:53.725
The people that you invested
in having something bad

00:17:53.725 --> 00:17:56.076
happen to them, so it slows you down.

00:17:56.076 --> 00:18:00.450
My friends, there are a
million reasons why not,

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:02.969
but there's one great reason why,

00:18:02.969 --> 00:18:05.349
which is, you've just got to persevere,

00:18:05.349 --> 00:18:06.605
no matter what it is.

00:18:06.605 --> 00:18:07.691
It's just the way it is.

00:18:07.691 --> 00:18:09.283
It's hard being an entrepreneur.

00:18:09.283 --> 00:18:11.053
It's hard building a business.

00:18:11.053 --> 00:18:13.556
Everybody thinks it's so easy
that there's an entitlement.

00:18:13.556 --> 00:18:14.398
There's a disaster.

00:18:14.398 --> 00:18:15.624
Zinging China?

00:18:15.624 --> 00:18:17.373
Here comes my US zing right now.

00:18:17.373 --> 00:18:20.273
There is an insane generation
of 18- to 25-year-olds

00:18:20.273 --> 00:18:22.701
right now that think they're
entitled to having a business

00:18:22.701 --> 00:18:25.081
because they saw the Social Network movie,

00:18:25.081 --> 00:18:26.628
and everybody's decided if you're a kid

00:18:26.628 --> 00:18:27.958
and you know what tech is,

00:18:27.958 --> 00:18:30.273
because you used Instagram early on,

00:18:30.273 --> 00:18:32.566
you're entitled to
actually build a business.

00:18:32.566 --> 00:18:34.582
Building a business is hard,

00:18:34.582 --> 00:18:36.752
and you know what makes it really hard?

00:18:36.752 --> 00:18:39.727
Everything that happens
every day of every moment.

00:18:39.727 --> 00:18:40.990
So.

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:44.493
You can pick time, you can
pick money, as the one or two

00:18:44.493 --> 00:18:47.148
things that you think stops
you from winning your game,

00:18:47.148 --> 00:18:49.922
but the truth is, there's
a million reasons.

00:18:49.922 --> 00:18:53.064
99% of businesses go out
of business for a reason,

00:18:53.064 --> 00:18:55.572
and that reason is, it's hard.

00:18:55.572 --> 00:18:58.135
And so, if you're watching this show,

00:18:58.135 --> 00:18:59.926
I've got a sense of who you are,

00:18:59.926 --> 00:19:02.391
and you need to start
creating layers and layers

00:19:02.391 --> 00:19:06.904
and layers and layers of skin
to be able to get through,

00:19:06.904 --> 00:19:10.998
because the glamour of
being an entrepreneur,

00:19:10.998 --> 00:19:12.580
though, goodness, you
know, you get very confused

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:14.503
by my optimism 'cause it's my optimism,

00:19:14.503 --> 00:19:15.845
I can't help it, it's just how I roll,

00:19:15.845 --> 00:19:17.696
it's probably one of the
variable one percent reasons

00:19:17.696 --> 00:19:21.451
why I'm successful, but
please, don't get it twisted.

00:19:21.451 --> 00:19:22.635
This is hard.

00:19:22.635 --> 00:19:23.832
Every day is hard.

00:19:23.832 --> 00:19:27.433
And if you don't have the
stomach to weather the storm,

00:19:27.433 --> 00:19:29.231
you will not be
successful, and by the way,

00:19:29.231 --> 00:19:31.598
let me throw you a real weird curve ball.

00:19:31.598 --> 00:19:33.086
And that's okay.

00:19:33.086 --> 00:19:34.464
People have to look
themselves in the mirror

00:19:34.464 --> 00:19:36.176
and understand if they're a
number two, three, four, five,

00:19:36.176 --> 00:19:38.172
six, seven in an organization that has

00:19:38.172 --> 00:19:40.487
differences of being a number one,

00:19:40.487 --> 00:19:43.201
but maybe that's where your skillset sits,

00:19:43.201 --> 00:19:46.557
maybe that's how you make
your fortunes and happiness

00:19:46.557 --> 00:19:49.490
and all the things that
you're looking for, and so...

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:51.613
That question got me
going a little bit, Steve,

00:19:51.613 --> 00:19:54.522
because it's under the context of excuses,

00:19:54.522 --> 00:19:56.747
and I will never make an excuse.

00:19:56.747 --> 00:19:58.535
Everything that's a problem with me,

00:19:58.535 --> 00:20:00.066
everything I don't achieve,

00:20:00.066 --> 00:20:02.429
everything that's the
problem with VaynerMedia,

00:20:02.429 --> 00:20:04.979
everything is my fault.

00:20:04.979 --> 00:20:05.925
And I...

00:20:05.925 --> 00:20:07.921
I succumb to that, and I respect that,

00:20:07.921 --> 00:20:09.713
and I actually think that's
the way it should be,

00:20:09.713 --> 00:20:10.546
and so...

00:20:11.757 --> 00:20:13.006
No excuses, my friends.

